# Untitled

## Two-dimensional d−π conjugated metal−organic framework based on hexahydroxytrinaphthylene
Zheng Meng and Katherine A. Mirica ( $\boxed{\boxtimes}$ )
Department of Chemistry, Burke Laboratory, 41 College Street, Dartmouth College, Hanover, NH 03755, USA
$\circledcirc$ Tsinghua University Press and Springer-Verlag GmbH Germany, part of Springer Nature 2020   
Received: 20 January 2020 / Revised: 30 April 2020 / Accepted: 11 May 2020
## ABSTRACT
The development of new two-dimensional (2D) ${\mathsf{d}}{-}{\boldsymbol{\pi}}$ conjugated metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) holds great promise for the construction of a new generation of porous and semiconductive materials. This paper describes the synthesis, structural characterization, and electronic properties of a new ${\mathsf{d}}{-}{\boldsymbol{\pi}}$ conjugated 2D MOF based on the use of a new ligand 2,3,8,9,14,15- hexahydroxytrinaphthylene. The reticular self-assembly of this large $\pi$ -conjugated organic building block with Cu(II) ions in a mixed solvent system of 1,3-dimethyl-2-imidazolidinone (DMI) and $H_{2}O$ with the addition of ammonia water or ethylenediamine leads to a highly crystalline MOF $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T N})_{2}$ , which possesses pore aperture of $2.5\mathsf{n m}$ . $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T N})_{2}$ MOF shows moderate electrical conductivity of $9.01\times10^{-8}{\mathsf{S}}{\cdot}{\mathsf{c m}}^{-1}$ at $385~\mathsf{K}$ and temperature-dependent band gap ranging from 0.75 to $1.65\mathrm{~eV}$ . After chemical oxidation by $\mathsf{I}_{2}$ , the conductivity of $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T N})_{2}$ can be increased by 360 times. This access to HHTN based MOF adds an important member to previously reported MOF systems with hexagonal lattice, paving the way towards systematic studies of structure–property relationships of semiconductive MOFs.
## KEYWORDS
two-dimensional, metal–organic framework (MOF), trinaphthylene, conductive MOF
## Introduction
The recent emergence of conductive metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) [1–4] offers opportunities for molecular engineering of novel functions in electronics [5–7], chemical sensing [8–19], magnetics [20–24], catalysis [25–30], and energy storage [31–37]. Characterized by well-defined modular structure accessible through bottom-up self-assembly, this class of materials provides tunable approach towards fundamental advances in multifunctional materials design [4]. The attractive feature of conductive MOFs centers on their elegant combination of intrinsic porosity and electrical conductivity, which differentiates them from conventional insulating MOFs [8–10]. Creation of efficient and long-range charge transport pathway typically relies on the construction of two-dimensional (2D) MOFs comprising extended $\pi$ -conjugated organic building blocks interconnected with transition metals [3, 38], where the $\mathtt{p-d}$ orbital coupling between the ligand and metal node facilitates “through-bond” charge transport [3, 4, 39]. Following this strategy, 2D MOFs with conductivity from semiconductive to metallic have been developed. Despite the applied and fundamental potential of 2D d–π conjugated MOFs, in most cases, strategic modulation of both the conductivity and porosity has remained limited, and the design principles for engineering conductivity and band gap have been largely unexplored.
With a few exceptions [16, 24, 28, 29, 40], the existing 2D conductive MOFs rely mainly on two types of organic building blocks: hexasubstituted benzene (HXB) [5, 41–44] and hexasubstituted triphenylene (HXTP, $\mathrm{X}=\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}$ , O, S, Se) [3, 4, 9, 25, 38, 42, 45–49]. These redox-active ligands with orthopositioned N, O, S, or Se donor atoms can coordinate to a variety of late-transition-metal nodes capable of providing octahedral or square-planar coordination geometry, resulting in nanoporous 2D MOFs with pore diameters ranging between $1{-}2~\mathrm{nm}$ . The increasing demand for the development of novel conductive MOFs, as well as the need for improving understanding of their structure–property relationships, can significantly benefit from the systematic expansion of the known structural topology of the Kagome lattice, with electronic structure, pore size and other properties controlled in an atomically precise manner. The construction of isoreticular series of MOFs, in which the MOFs share the same 2D topology, but possess different functionalities and dimensions, has proven to be a powerful strategy to probe the structure–property relationships [50–53]. This strategy leverages the fact that the structure of the organic ligand can directly influence the size of pore aperture and efficiency of the electronic communication between ligand and metal nodes [3, 4, 39, 54].
In this paper, we describe the synthesis, structural characterization, and properties of a new ${\mathrm{d}}-\pi$ conjugated 2D MOF based on a new ligand—2,3,8,9,14,15- hexahydroxytrinaphthylene (HHTN)—the $\pi$ -extended analog of 1,2,3,4,5,6-hexahydroxybenzene (HHB) and 2,3,6,7,10,11- hexahydroxytriphenylene (HHTP). The reticular self-assembly of this large $\pi$ -conjugated organic building block with $\operatorname{Cu}(\operatorname{II})$ ions leads to a MOF $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTN})_{2}$ with pore aperture up to $2.5~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , pushing the 2D $\pi$ -conjugated semi-conductive MOF into the mesoporous realm. $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTN})_{2}$ showed moderate electrical conductivity of $9.01\times10^{-8}\ \mathrm{S{\cdot}c m^{-1}}$ at $385\mathrm{~K~}$ and temperature-dependent band gaps ranging from 0.75 to $1.65\ \mathrm{eV.}$
After chemical oxidation by ${\mathrm{I}}_{2},$ the conductivity of $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTN})_{2}$ can be increased by 360 times, highlighting the tunability of the property. The access to HHTN-based MOF adds an important member to previously reported HHB- and HHTP-based MOFs, thus generating the first isoreticular family of 2D d–π conjugated MOFs comprising at least three systematically expanded lattice structures. This achievement can promote comparative fundamental study of structure–property relationships in 2D ${\bf d}-\pi$ conjugated MOFs, and is poised to pave the way for new opportunities in thermal switching, sensing, and energy-related applications.
## 2 Results and discussion
## 2.1 MOF synthesis
## 2.2 Structural characterization
The formation of $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTN})_{2}\mathrm{MOF}$ with the structure consistent with that in Fig. 2(a) was confirmed by powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD). As shown in Fig. 2(b), the bulk material $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTN})_{2}$ showed prominent peaks at $2\theta=3.49^{\circ}$ , $6.95^{\circ}$ , $9.17^{\circ}$ , $12.02^{\circ}$ , and $27.13^{\circ}$ , corresponding to the diffractions of [100], [200], [210], [220], and [001] planes, respectively. From peaks at these values, a structural pore diameter (defined by the distance of the hydrogen atoms at the opposite sides of the hexagon) of $2.5\ \mathrm{nm}$ was deduced based on Bragg’s law. This value is much larger than the pore diameters of HXTP- and HXB-based MOFs, which have been reported to be 1.8–2.0 [9, 31, 45, 46] and $0.8\mathrm{nm}$ [34, 43], respectively. The presence of the sharp diffraction peaks from the planes perpendicular to ab planes indicated the long-range hexagonal in-plane arrangement. The diffraction peak of [001] at $27.13^{\circ}$ was relatively weak, demonstrating a diminished long-range order along the $\boldsymbol{c}$ direction. The interlayer spacing $d_{001}$ calculated from the [001] diffraction was $3.29\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , which is similar with that of the $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})_{2}$ $\ Q_{001}=3.3\ \mathring\mathrm{A}$ ) [13, 38], but larger than that of $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHB})_{2}$ ( $\dot{Q}_{001}=2.96\mathring\mathrm{A})$ [44].
To ascertain the packing mode, three possible types of structural models, including fully eclipsed, slipped parallel, and staggered packing, were computationally examined for $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTN})_{2}$ . Density functional theory (DFT) optimization was used for the fully eclipsed model to optimize the atomic coordinates. To determine the propensity for slip plane, 78 structures with different $a b$ -plane displacements were generated from the fully eclipsed model. The free energies of the resulting structures were analyzed and compared using single-point energy calculations based on molecular force field COMPASS II (ultrafine level) with FORCIET modular implemented in Materials Studio. As shown in Fig. 2(c), for $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTN})_{2}$ MOF, the minimum of the potential energy surface corresponds to a displacement $\mathrm{i}a$ and $\Delta b$ ) of $1.{\overset{-}{4}}\mathrm{-}1.5{\overset{\circ}{\mathrm{~A~}}}$ along the $a$ axis and $\overset{\cdot}{0.7}\substack{-0.8\mathrm{~\AA~}}$ along the $b$ axis between the adjacent two layers (Fig. 2(d)).
The same method was also used for the determination of the displacement for $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHB})_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})_{2}.$ , which have also been previously characterized to have slipped parallel packing modes [44, 47, 56]. The results showed that displacements along $a$ and $b$ axes in the most thermodynamically stable lattices were $\Delta a=2.5\textup{\AA}.$ , $\Delta b=1.2\textrm{\AA}$ for $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHB})_{2}$ and $\Delta a=3.7\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , $\Delta b=3.0\mathrm{~\AA~}$ for $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})_{2}$ (Figs. S14 and S15 in the ESM). This comparison illustrated that the relative displacement in these three MOFs ( $\dot{\Delta a}/a$ and $\Delta b/b\dot{}$ ) contracts with the extension of the $\pi$ -system of the ligand. This result suggests that $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions between adjacent layers gradually increase with the expansion of the ligand and have a dominant effect on the interlayer packing, considering that interlayer displacement is a function of the balance between the attractive $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions, interlayer metal–oxygen interactions, and electrostatic repulsions resulting from oxygens and metal centers in the adjacent layers [57].
$\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTN})_{2}$ exhibited a reversible nitrogen sorption isotherm curves with a Brunauer–Emmett–Teller surface area of $486~\mathrm{m^{2}{\cdot}g^{-1}}$ (Fig. S24 in the ESM), which is 3.4 times greater than that of $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHB})_{2}$ [44] and 1.7 times greater than that of $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})_{2}$ [58]. The distribution of accessible pore size derived from the gas adsorption centered around $2\ \mathrm{nm}$ . This value is smaller than that deduced from PXRD analysis, which can be due to the slipped parallel packing that reduces the accessible size of the channel, and the possible presence of counter ions and other adsorbed species (e.g., solvent molecules).
## 2.3 Morphological characterization
Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) analysis of $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTN})_{2}$ showed sub-micrometer sized rods (Figs. 3(a) and 3(b)). Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) imaging of $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTN})_{2}$ revealed sheet-like morphology with multiple layers (Fig. 3(c)). TEM image at higher magnification at the edge of the MOF sheet allowed the visualization of regular stripes with a spacing of $1.29\mathrm{nm}$ corresponding to [200] facets (Fig. 3(d)); this value was in good agreement with the value obtained from the PXRD analysis $(d_{200}=1.27\mathrm{nm}$ ). The ease of visualization of the [200] facets compared with facets aligned in other directions is likely due to the preferential orientation of the observed crystallite on the TEM grid or acquisition conditions for the TEM image [38]. These imaging results further evidenced the formation of $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTN})_{2}$ MOF with high structural regularity at micro and nanoscale.
## 2.4 Chemical analysis
Energy dispersive spectroscopy (Fig. S21 in the ESM) conducted for corresponding areas showed the presence of C, O, and Cu elements, consistent with the desired composition of $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTN})_{2}$ MOF. High-resolution scans by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) of the O 1s showed three peaks with binding energies at 284.7, 286.2, and $288.1\ \mathrm{eV}$ (Fig. S10(a) in the ESM), ascribed to carbon in the environments of C–C, C–O, and $\mathrm{C=O}.$ . The 1:1 relative intensity of the $C{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{O}$ to $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ suggested that the oxidation state of the ligand is likely to exist as tri(semiquinone) form, corresponding to $^{-3}$ apparent charge. This analysis was consistent with the analysis of the highresolution scan of O 1s range, which can be fitted to two peaks for $C{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{O}$ and $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ at binding energies of 531.5 and $532.9\mathrm{eV}$ in an intensity ratio of 48:52 (Fig. S10(c) in the ESM). Highresolution scan of $\mathrm{Cu}2\mathrm{p}$ range can be deconvoluted into two components at binding energies of 933.5 and $935.3\mathrm{eV}$ with an intensity ratio of 41:59 (Fig. S10(d) in the ESM), suggesting that $\mathrm{Cu}$ in $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTN})_{2}$ may exist in a mixed oxidation state [9] of $\mathrm{{Cu(I)}}$ and $\mathrm{{Cu(II)}}$ in a ratio of 2:3. The presence of $\operatorname{Cu}(\operatorname{II})$ was also confirmed by the electron paramagnetic resonance spectroscopy in which a strong metal-centered radical ascribed to paramagnetic $\mathrm{{Cu}(I I)}$ was observed (Fig. S22 in the ESM). The above analysis suggested that the skeleton of the MOF with a formula $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTN})_{2}$ is likely to possess a net charge of nearly $^{-1}$ . The framework may achieve charge neutrality by the incorporation of counter ions, that are very likely from the $\mathrm{NH_{4}^{+}}$ introduced from the MOF synthesis, as suggested by the presence of $\mathrm{\DeltaN}$ in XPS (Fig. S10(b) in the ESM).
## 2.5 Electronic properties and tunability
Given the ${\mathrm{d}}-\pi$ conjugated structure of $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTN})_{2}$ MOF, we proceeded to examine its electronic properties. Ultraviolet– visible–near-infrared (UV–vis–NIR) spectroscopy revealed a relatively broad absorption band, which tailed into the NIR region beyond $850~\mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 4(a)). The optical band gap was estimated to be $1.61\ \mathrm{eV}$ based on the value of absorption edge (inset in Fig. 4(a)). The bulk conductivity of $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTN})_{2}$ MOF was measured on a pressed pellet with a thickness of $\sim1~\mathrm{mm}$ by a two-point method, which gave a value of $9.55\times10^{-10}$ $\mathsf{S}{\cdot}\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ at room temperature. Variable temperature conductivity measurement showed that increasing the temperature from 298 to $385~\mathrm{K}$ increased the conductivity to $9.01\times10^{-8}\mathrm{{S}{\cdot}\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}}$ (Fig. 4(b)), which is a 94-fold increase within a $\Delta T$ of $87~\mathrm{K}$ .
The Arrhenius fit of conductivity to temperature revealed two distinct regions (Fig. 4(c)), demonstrating a notable temperature dependence of band gap within the tested temperature range. The activation energy for the transport of charge carriers in $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTN})_{2}$ MOF determined at a lower temperature range of 298–319 K (zone I in Fig. 4(c)) was $827~\mathrm{meV}$ (Fig. S27 in the ESM), corresponding to a thermal bandgap of $1.65\mathrm{eV};$ ; this bandgap value is in good agreement with the value estimated by UV–vis–NIR spectroscopy. In the higher temperature range of 324–385 K (zone II in Fig. 4(c)), a smaller thermal band gap of $0.75\mathrm{eV}$ was found (Fig. S27 in the ESM).
Although temperature-dependence of band gap has been well documented in traditional inorganic semiconductors [59–64], and has recently been recognized in organic semiconductors [65, 66] and perovskites [67], such as Si, Ge, poly(3,4- ethylenedioxythiophene) and $(\mathrm{C_{4}H_{9}N H_{3}})_{2}(\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}})_{n-1}\mathrm{Pb}_{n}\mathrm{I}_{3n+1},$ this property has not been extensively characterized in semiconducting reticular materials. Theoretically, the energy barrier for charge transport is ascribed to both the intrinsic charge transport within the crystallite (intraparticle transport) and charge hopping over grain boundaries (interparticle transport) in the bulk measurement [43]. The relatively minor contribution from grain boundary effects to the total energy barrier of charge transport, as evidenced by the fact that the thermal band gap obtained at room temperature is only slightly higher than optical band gap for $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTN})_{2},$ suggested that the temperature-dependence of band gap was mainly caused by intrinsic changes in charge transport barrier [68, 69].
Compared to $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})_{2}$ [8, 13, 38, 47] and $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHB})_{2}$ [44] MOFs, $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTN})_{2}$ showed a widened electronic band gap and lower conductivity, which might be due to the weaker orbital interaction between $\mathrm{{Cu(II)}}$ and organic ligand [39, 44, 70] and the relatively larger free void space along the ab plane that is not favorable for in-plane charge transport [54]. Another reason for these properties, however, could be the oxidation state of the ligand or the metal within the MOF scaffold, responsible for the negatively charged reduced state of the materials as evidenced by the inferred presence of positively charged $\mathrm{NH_{4}^{+}}$ counterions [71, 72].
Inspired by previous reports that the electrical properties of reticular materials composed of redox-active units can be modulated by partial oxidation or reduction through chemical doping [71–73], we further investigated the effect of oxidation on the electrical conductivity of $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTN})_{2}$ by doping with iodine. A resistor device made by drop-casting the MOF suspension on a gold interdigitated electrode $5\upmu\mathrm{m}$ gap) was exposed to $\mathrm{I}_{2}$ stream $(\sim400~\mathrm{ppm})$ at room temperature (see Section S11 in the ESM). Exposure of the material to the stream of $\mathrm{I}_{2}$ for about $30~\mathrm{min}$ increased the conductance of the device by $36,000\%$ . The doping process was partially reversible: Stopping $\mathrm{I}_{2}$ exposure reversed the current to 157-folds of the original value within $30\ \mathrm{min}$ . XPS characterization showed that $\mathrm{I}_{2}$ doping process likely involved the partial oxidation of both the ligand and metal in the MOF material (Fig. S30 in the ESM) and the formation of the ${\mathrm{I}}_{3}^{-}$ ions within the MOFs. PXRD of ${\mathrm{I}}_{2}$ treated sample exhibited two obvious peaks at $2\theta=3.5^{\circ}$ and $7.0^{\circ}{}_{;}$ , consistent with the [100] and [200] peaks of pristine $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTN})_{2},$ indicating that $\mathrm{I}_{2}$ doped $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTN})_{2}$ still maintained the crystalline framework structure. The peak broadening of [100] and [200] diffractions in the $\mathrm{I}_{2}$ treated $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTN})_{2}$ sample may be due to the possible formation of crystalline ${\mathrm{I}}_{3}^{-}$ chains inside the channel that caused the reordering of the MOFs layers. Electron paramagnetic resonance (EPR) studies showed a new signal with a $g$ value at 1.994 for the iodine-doped $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTN})_{2}$ sample (Fig. S31 in the ESM), indicating the formation of ligand-centered radical. Therefore, the increased electrical conductivity of the iodine-doped sample may be due to the improvement in oxidation-induced orbital overlap between the ligand and metal, as well as the formation of free charge carriers through oxidative doping [72]. The increase in conductance upon oxidative doping of $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTN})_{2}$ MOF suggested a p-type semiconductive character of this material.
## 3 Conclusions
We have designed and synthesized a new $\mathrm{d}-\pi$ conjugated 2D MOF based on the utilization of a new hexatopic ligand HHTN, which is a $\pi$ -extended analog for HHB and HHTP ligands. The reticular incorporation of this large $\pi$ -conjugated organic building block with $\mathrm{cu}$ ions leads to a $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTN})_{2}$ MOF with a pore aperture of $2.5\mathrm{nm}$ , extending the porosity of 2D $\pi$ -conjugated semiconductive MOFs into the mesoporous realm. $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTN})_{2}$ showed low to moderate electrical conductivity of $9.01\times10^{-8}\mathrm{{S}{\cdot}\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}}$ at $385~\mathrm{K}$ and temperaturedependent electrical band gap ranging from 1.65 to $0.75\ \mathrm{eV}.$ After chemical oxidation by ${\mathrm{I}}_{2},$ the conductivity of $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTN})_{2}$ can be improved by 360 times. These results demonstrated the great tunability in conductivity of $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTN})_{2}$ MOF using both thermal and chemical stimuli. Despite a computational proposal for achieving a trinaphthylene-based MOF by expanding the size of the ligand [74], the first experimental access and characterization of $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTN})_{2}$ MOF reported herein constitutes the first experimentally accessible trinaphthylenebased $\mathrm{d}-\pi$ conjugated MOF. Thus, our work adds an important member to the previously reported HHB- and HHTP-based MOFs, generating the first isoreticular family of 2D d–π conjugated MOFs comprising at least three systematically expanded lattice structures. The lower conductivity and higher porosity found in $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTN})_{2}$ compared with HHB- and HHTP-based analogs, suggested that porosity and conductivity, which are two important parameters for many electrochemical applications [75], need to be balanced in the design of conductive MOFs. Achieving this delicate balance involves the consideration of several interdependent factors, which can be potentially addressed through a judicious choice of organic ligand and metal node to provide a high-degree orbital overlap of the building blocks, post-synthetic modifications, including doping, redox control, and guest infiltration [71–73, 75, 76]. We believe these results may promote future studies of systematic structure–property relationships by examining the 2D $\mathrm{d}-\pi$ conjugated series of MOFs experimentally to aid the understanding, design, and realization of electrically conductive MOFs, and may offer new opportunities for exploring this class of materials in thermal switching, sensing, and energy-related applications.
